(ns kotoba.compiler.nbb.host.hash
  "MECHANISM LAYER -- the nbb/Node handler of the hash/sha256 ability
  (catalog wire 3; superproject adr-2609242330 S5, section 1 (c) and
  section 3). Not Kotoba source: a compiler module writes
  `(hash/sha256 value)` and requires this namespace only under
  `#?(:cljs ...)`. The answer is 64 lowercase hex characters -- the kexe
  loader's provider shape.

  Why an ability and not the pure library here: org-nist-sha2 under nbb is
  measured at roughly three orders of magnitude slower than node:crypto
  (adr-2609242330 S1: 185 ms against 0.07 ms for 100 KiB), and the compiler
  hashes every artifact, provenance text and cache key it produces. The pure
  library is what a target without a native digest answers with; the value
  is the same, and scripts/test-nbb-host-abilities.cljk pins that.

  VALUE is a string (hashed as UTF-8) or a byte buffer -- the two request
  kinds the compiler has, `:string` and `:bytes`. See
  kotoba.compiler.nbb.host.io."
  {:kotoba/export []}
  (:require ["node:crypto" :as crypto]))

(defn sha256
  "hash/sha256 3."
  [value]
  (-> (.createHash crypto "sha256") (.update value) (.digest "hex")))
